)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+20\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331042/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331042"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331043/selection/4d718022eb8af437ca4fbb01bbc8ad43cb2837048e8bb4d9a0693b94dd945ad3" :answer/id #uuid "22b81cf8-5315-556c-b103-9a24b2d64080" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331043/selection/16280c26960d820ef36dde3d23d657d6b1c3179c69e5ade612acf24d6fbfbf89" :answer/id #uuid "82d1043c-f4e2-594b-a2d3-5a0e3bf57664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331043/select