unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331043/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331043"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331032/selection/1b784fb596139bdf9478509633ae3776bb5b083fc637831e9f69ec80ec385620" :answer/id #uuid "e7e6bbeb-f444-5967-862c-d6b32af19b3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331032/selection/16a07df297945888bda5c515558835647cd4c95b2d7aa6050202f4bfd22749cc" :answer/id #uuid 