h-academy-id "q-342637" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(5(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342637/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342637"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342318"] :question/problem "Find $L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)={e}^{-2(t-3)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-2)}=\\frac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3){e}^{-2(t-3)}} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-342631/selection/c8ba91514af75b2dc6dca79864927e0b83ea3533a8b500c4e222d7fda35dfdaf" :answer/id #uuid "cc09700e-29c9-557c-9733-4268dca07345" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342631/selection/1735b3fb79bdd9120b0051e76a1140f1cda57410b4fd8ae391e7339893cf1112" :answer/id #uuid "729cc97a-76a6-5eeb-99bb-d57a89aaddc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342631/selection/300261e8ed156c334a31d588284d50b189a5012767283399c57f495da5a6d43f" :answer/id #uuid "4341dea8-4422-5a12-8fcb-7fd57b2c9e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342631/selection/c73db885f7e590d2fdd5a4b7ef763529918e07b47c9cda09db399f95072ad38e" :answer/id #uuid "bb2c6993-4eb3-5270-8757-155b1544aa47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342631/selection/139d7816b49493481940885780225844080c7a2ee47c1590b771a5e9b994bd6a" :answer/id #uuid "9f46c5d7-828c-51