(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4))} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342638/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342638"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection/3c0d6312387e069bbbe2af6ef5b56210f5e58b6de032b85857357b32f5cb2c4e" :answer/id #uuid "a2591dcd-93c5-5656-aa37-350a560ac32e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection/7c6e7c54f499f2f0a47c19f1b0c3e8ff7bf32f51fd6a12291d6825b46ef8f82d" :answer/id #uuid "4183603e-6b43-5fe5-a7ae-46bc68610604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection/df9aa8641cd41986f77278e0baacfa36e5acaca3246822cb8d4bd710bb556f90" :answer/id #uuid "f2282be5-5304-5abc-8345-7f906cd536cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection/e59f27369ad0a5216cb955661070fc5dfcfd0ec6b48e8841741f7ff1571764a7" :answer/id #uuid "1c6150df-446f-53bb-a790-8f035789b6b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection/fd14978df5ba2edf5ba9116f2350a388819435a766e467eadab6a4a15f623450" :answer/id #uuid "a806298c-9601-5fb1-946b-0f664b15063f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0546e4f-2b43-55d5-8a96-4eed1f72fcfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342626/selection/3c0d6312387e069bbbe2af6ef5b56210f5e58b6de032b85857357b32f5cb2c4e" :answer-field/choices ["q-342626/selection/3c0d6312387e069bbbe2af6ef5b56210f5e58b6de032b85857357b32f5cb2c4e" "q-342626/selection/7c6e7c54f499f2f0a47c19f1b0c3e8ff7bf32f51fd6a12291d6825b46ef8f82d" "q-342626/selection/df9aa8641cd41986f77278e0baacfa36e5acaca3246822cb8d4bd710bb556f90" "q-342626/selection/e59f27369ad0a5216cb955661070fc5dfcfd0ec6b48e8841741f7ff1571764a7" "q-342626/selection/fd14978df5ba2edf5ba9116f2350a388819435a766e467eadab6a4a15f623450"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342626" :question/id #uuid "9e9f0adc-a25d-5538-9d91-d02b372d3966" :question/math-academy-id "q-342626" :question/problem "Find $L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)={e}^{-5(t-4)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342626/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21445"] :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{{t}^{2}+7t+12} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+7L{t}+12L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21582"] :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21864"] :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1)(t-1)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)(t-1)^{3}} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
